  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1243 PM CDT Sun Jun 14 2026\n\n18z synopsis shows a large longwave trough across much of the \nnorthern plains leading to northwest flow aloft through the central \nplains.  At the surface an area of high pressure is building across \nthe front range of the Rockies which is allowing strong cold air \nadvection for this time of year across much of western Kansas.\n\nTonight and into Monday morning with continued northwest flow aloft \nshort term models have two upper level shortwaves coming out of \neastern Colorado.  The one for late tonight has an area of rain \ndeveloping along the front range and moving into southeast Colorado \naround sunset.  The models are hinting at this line losing upper \nlevel lift by the time it gets to Kansas so most of the rain should \nsubside by the state line.  A second wave from northeast Colorado \nwill move into northwest and north central Kansas Monday morning. \nTHis upper level lift could lead to some isolated rain showers along \nthe I-70 corridor between sunrise and noon however with low \nconfidence in any one location receiving rain I kept POPs at around \n10-20%.\n\n \nMonday should be slightly warmer with the return of southerly flow \nnear the surface and 850 mb temperatures reaching to around 18 (C). \nHighs should make it into the 80s.  Late Monday afternoon into the \nevening another 500-700 mb shortwave is expected to move from \nnortheast Colorado to central Kansas.  CAMs and other short term \nmodels think that this lift along with higher amounts of lower level \nmoisture will lead to pockets of showers and thunderstorms for \nmainly areas along and west of highway 283 during the evening and \novernight hours on Monday.\n\nWednesday as a strong ridge builds in the western CONUS we should \nsee a pocket of hot air aided by southwest winds that will move into \nsouthwest Kansas during the day.  Forecast 850 mb temperatures will \nrise to around 29-32 (C) and with an approaching cold front \nWednesday afternoon to aid compressional heating...we should see \nhighs reach into the 100s with NBM probabilities of 100 degree \nweather in the 70-80% range for all of southwest Kansas.  Forecast \nhighs of 103 in Dodge and Garden City will make a run at the record \nhigh of 104 and a forecast high of 106 in Medicine Lodge will make a \nrun at the record high of 107.  The air should be drier across \nsouthwest Kansas so heat index values will be less than the \ntemperature with the exception of Barber county which is forecast to \nbe in heat advisory criteria.  The heat event should be short lived \nas cooler air returns on Thursday.",
